hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-26 = cons "This is how a knowledge graph should represent incomplete knowledge. A source that reports something is an" (cons "observation. What follows from the report is whatever holds for every state of affairs compatible with it. Missing" (cons "information is kept as the family of possibilities, not compressed into a number. Combining two sources intersects" (cons "their fibers, and refining an observation shrinks the fiber, so a conclusion established on the coarser fiber stays" (cons "established on the finer one. When a probability structure really is given, it is extra information that further" (cons "reasoning can use. It isn't a stand-in for the information that's absent." (cons "" (cons "This settles the open-world versus closed-world split that divided the semantic web from databases. Open-world" (cons "reasoning says absence of a fact isn't its negation. Closed-world reasoning says it is. In hyperactivity, absence is" (cons (quoted (cons "itself an observation with a fiber. A dependency certificate (§10.5 " (cons "Dependency certificates and persistent" nil))) (cons (quoted (cons "evaluation" (cons ") records a lookup that found nothing as a dependency on that absence at a given revision. So a" nil))) (cons "conclusion drawn from absence is valid exactly while the absence holds, and the graph knows when it stops holding." (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "The same construction governs action (§10.4 " (cons "Observations, information, and locally available actions" (cons ", equation" nil)))) (cons "10.11). An action is safe at an observation when its condition holds over every compatible state:" (cons "" (cons "  SafeAt(y) := Π_{(s,p):Fiber_o(y)} P(s)." (cons "" (cons "A knowledge graph that drives decisions (in an organization, an agent or an instrument) needs exactly this: the" (cons "evidence that a decision is justified by what is actually known, not by a guess about the hidden state." (cons "" nil))))))))))))))))))))
